      "text": "We start by inserting the matrices into the multiplication:\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\dotsm \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 99 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n\nWe take the first two matrices and compute their product first:\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\cdot 1 + 1 \\cdot 0 & 1 \\cdot 3 + 1 \\cdot 1 \\\\ 0 \\cdot 1 + 1 \\cdot 0 & 0 \\cdot 3 + 1 \\cdot 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n\nNext, we take this result and multiply it by the next matrix:\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\cdot 1 + 4 \\cdot 0 & 1 \\cdot 5 + 4 \\cdot 1 \\\\ 0 \\cdot 1 + 1 \\cdot 0 & 0 \\cdot 5 + 1 \\cdot 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n\nWe observe a pattern here. The product of any two consecutive matrices of the form\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & a \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & b \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & a + b \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n\nTherefore, we can write the product of all matrices as:\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 + 3 + 5 + \\cdots + 99 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n\nWe need to find the sum of the first 50 odd numbers, which are \\(1, 3, 5, \\ldots, 99\\). The sum of the first \\(n\\) odd numbers is \\(n^2\\). Here, \\(n = 50\\), so the sum is:\n\\[1 + 3 + 5 + \\cdots + 99 = 50^2 = 2500.\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2500 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n\nTherefore, the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2500 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}}\\).",
