      "text": "To solve the given matrix product, we start by examining the general form of the matrices involved. Each matrix in the product is of the form:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & a_i \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(a_i = 2i - 1\\) for \\(i = 1, 2, \\ldots, 50\\). Therefore, the product of these matrices is:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\dotsm \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 99 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nWe can use the property of matrix multiplication to simplify this. Notice that the product of matrices of the form \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & b_i \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is another matrix of the same form, scaled by the product of the numbers 1 through 50. Specifically, the element in the first row and first column of the product is:\n\n\\[\n1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot 1 - 1 \\cdot 3 - 1 \\cdot 5 - \\ldots - 1 \\cdot 99 = 1 - (1 + 3 + 5 + \\ldots + 99)\n\\]\n\nThe sum of the first 50 odd numbers is \\(1 + 3 + 5 + \\ldots + 99 = 50^2 = 2500\\). Therefore, the element in the first row and first column is:\n\n\\[\n1 - 2500 = -2499\n\\]\n\nThe element in the second row and second column is:\n\n\\[\n1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot 1 = 1\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of the matrices is:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} -2499 & -2500 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -2499 & -2500 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]",
